+The proposed UltraFeedback panel retains the UltraChat, ShareGPT, and Evol-Instruct sources, excludes TruthfulQA, FalseQA, and FLAN sources, and removes benchmark overlaps before splitting by normalized prompt identity. Target sizes are $20$k/$2$k preference-model train/dev, $10$k/$1$k policy train/dev, and $2$k final evaluation. If filtering leaves $30$k--$35$k eligible unique prompts (excluding $35$k), policy training is reduced to $5$k before training begins; fewer than $30$k requires an explicitly revised data budget; exact retained counts and hashes must be reported. No final prompt is used for preference-model or policy selection.
+
+Released GPT-4 ratings train four attribute heads. Valid ratings induce $0/1$ pair labels, ties use $0.5$, and missing labels are masked. Four completions produce six unordered pairs per prompt. These labels are transitive by construction and do not demonstrate human preference cycles. Policy-training prompts are disjoint and use new base-policy responses; no new paid judge API is required to reuse the released annotations.
+
+The proposed GPM and scalar BT model share the 149M ModernBERT-base encoder with native $8192$-token support \citep{warner2024modernbert}. The GPM retains the antisymmetric construction above; BT uses differences of four scalar scores. They share supervision, one frozen preference-model seed, and prompt splits; policy-seed repeats do not measure teacher uncertainty. Initial fine-tuning settings are AdamW, learning rate $2\times10^{-5}$, batch $32$ pairs, weight decay $0.01$, warmup $0.06$, and up to two epochs selected on preference-model development data. Lengths are bucketed, unsupported inputs are audited, and generated-policy evaluation reads the full response under a separate judge.
+
+The proposed policy recipe retains full 8B fine-tuning, batch $32$ pairs, and the existing peak learning-rate scale, with $1250$ updates for $10$k prompts or $625$ for $5$k. This fixes four sampled pair exposures per training prompt on average; it is not a claim that the budget is optimal. Each run uses its own cosine schedule and one outer stage. Proposal generation uses independent learner/reference pools of eight occurrences each. Reference--reference scoring and the shared tensor are cached across methods. With two encoder orders and four joint heads, $10$k prompts require $2\times10^4(64+28)=1.84$ million encoder passes; actual throughput, memory, and token counts determine the execution budget.
